      "writtenOn": "2019-08-19T18:46:22Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I don\u0027t understand this.  If we have 1.5*n+1.5 available, why would we do 3A+1.5A in the PD+non-PD case and only 1.5A+1.5A in the PD+PD case?\n\nThere are plenty of low-cost, large-battery devices that could take 3A to charge but not do PD, such as battery banks, low-cost phones (Android One in particular), cheap tablets.  Seems weird to not give them 3A, since Type-C explicitly supports dynamically swapping between 3A and 1.5A via resistors alone.\n\nHow about the following heuristic instead:\n1. First device attached gets 3A\n2A. If second device supports PD (and wants 3A), first device drops to 1.5A and 3A is given to second device (avoids issues with gnubby+phone combo)\n2B. If second device does not support PD, the first device keeps 3A and the second device gets 1.5A.",
      "revId": "f00b876034256403039546804049b81cfad09b7d",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
